h if and only if they are related by a sequence of mutations.\nTo make sense of this assertion, we deﬁne the notion of a share.\nDeﬁnition A share is a part of a chord diagram consisting of two arcs of the outer\ncircle with the following property: each chord one of whose ends belongs to these arcs\nhas both endpoints on these arcs.\nDeﬁnition A mutation of a chord diagram is another chord diagram obtained by the\nﬂip of a share.\nNote that the complement of a share is also a share. The fact that a share consists of\ntwo arcs should give some sort of restriction. Also, note that if there is a triangular cell in\nthe medial graph, ﬂipping the share also corresponds locally to a Y-Delta transformation.\n8--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA 2-isomorphism theorem for delta-matroids (https://pure.royalholloway.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/39545390/Whitney_PUBLISHED.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Four small circular chord-diagram sketches are shown in a row, each with the word “share” inside; the middle two appear shaded/gray and the outer two unshaded/white, illustrating mutation variants. ... If we define the intersection graph of a orientable bouquet to be the intersection graph of the corresponding chord diagram, then Theorem 4 immediately gives the following.\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 7: Mutation for chord diagrams. Four small circular chord-diagram sketches are shown in a row, each with the word “share” inside; the middle two appear shaded/gray and the outer two unshaded/white, illustrating mutation variants.\n</visual_element>\n\nThe idea behind the proof of this theorem is to use Cunningham’s theory of graph decompositions from [13] to obtain a decomposition of an intersection graph into ‘prime’ graphs that have unique intersection graphs. This gives a decomposition of a chord diagram into minimal shares. Mutation then corresponds to the choices that are made when reassem